A = [
dict(n=61, slug="ai-agent", title="What Makes an AI an \"Agent\"?", topic="AI agent",
buzz='"AI agents" are the buzzword of the moment. Every product is suddenly an agent. What actually makes something an agent?',
hw='A chatbot is like a librarian: you ask, it answers, done. An agent is like a personal assistant: you say "book me a meeting with Priya next week", and it checks calendars, picks a slot, sends the invite and tells you when it\'s done.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: an **AI agent** takes a goal, decides the steps itself, uses tools to act, and keeps going until the job is done.',
step='Under the hood, it\'s a loop: the AI thinks about the next step, calls a tool (write-up 63), reads the result, and decides again, until the goal is met or it gets stuck.\n\nThat freedom is the power and the risk: an agent can take wrong steps, loop, or act on bad information. What goes wrong in practice is the theme of my Series 2, "Beyond Just RAG: From Answers to Actions".',
met='Coding assistants that edit files and run tests. Assistants that book, order or file things for you.',
name='An **AI agent**.',
words=[("Agent","an AI that plans steps and acts with tools to reach a goal."),("Tool","something the agent can use: search, calendar, database."),("Agent loop","think, act, check, repeat.")],
notit='An agent isn\'t just a chatbot with a new name, and it doesn\'t need to be autonomous for everything. Many tasks are better as a fixed workflow.',
take=["Ask: does this task need the AI to choose its own steps?","Give agents only the tools they need.","Put a person in charge of irreversible actions."],
close="A chatbot answers. An agent gets it done.",
q="Which task would you hand to an AI agent first?",
scene='A librarian answers your question.\nA personal assistant books the meeting.',
hook="A chatbot is the librarian. An AI agent is the assistant who gets it done.",
extra="Goal in, steps chosen, tools used."),

dict(n=62, slug="agentic-ai", title="What Does \"Agentic\" Really Mean?", topic="Agentic AI",
buzz='"Agentic AI." "Agentic workflows." The word is on every keynote slide. Is it different from "AI agent"?',
hw='Think of a scale, not a switch. At one end: a calculator, which does exactly what you press. At the other: a trusted manager, who decides what to do and does it.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **agentic** describes how much an AI system decides and acts on its own. More agentic means more freedom over its own steps.',
step='A system can be a little agentic (an AI picks which of two tools to call) or very agentic (it plans a multi-day project and acts without asking). "Agentic AI" usually means systems towards the freer end, built around agents (write-up 61).\n\nMore freedom means more flexibility, but also less predictability, more cost and more need for limits and checks.',
met='Product launches promising "agentic" features, from email to coding to customer service.',
name='**Agentic AI**.',
words=[("Agentic","acting with some independence towards a goal."),("Autonomy","how much it decides without asking."),("Human in the loop","a person approving key steps.")],
notit='"Agentic" isn\'t a quality badge. More autonomy isn\'t always better; for routine work, a fixed workflow is often cheaper and safer (Series 2, write-up 6).',
take=["Ask \"how agentic?\" instead of \"is it agentic?\".","Match autonomy to the risk of the task.","Start with less freedom, then widen it as it earns trust."],
close="Agentic is a dial, not a switch.",
q="How much freedom would you give an AI at work?",
scene='A calculator does exactly what you press.\nA trusted manager decides and acts.',
hook="Agentic is how far along that scale an AI system sits.",
extra="It's a dial, not a switch."),

dict(n=63, slug="tool-calling", title="How Does an AI Check the Weather?", topic="Tool calling",
buzz='"Tool calling." "Function calling." It\'s how AI went from talking to doing. The words sound technical.',
hw='Ask an AI "What\'s the weather in Pune right now?" It can\'t know: its knowledge stopped at training (write-up 27). So it says, in effect: "Please run the weather tool for Pune." Your app runs it, gets "31°C, sunny", and hands it back. The AI replies: "It\'s 31°C and sunny in Pune."\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **tool calling** lets an AI ask your software to do something and use the result.',
step='You describe the available tools to the AI: their names, what they do and what they need. When a tool would help, the AI replies with a structured request (write-up 24) instead of an answer. Your code runs the tool and returns the result, and the AI continues.\n\nThe AI never runs anything itself; your software stays in control of what actually happens.',
met='Chatbots that search the web, check your calendar or look up an order. Every AI agent (write-up 61).',
name='**Tool calling**, also called **function calling**.',
words=[("Tool","a function or service the AI can request."),("Tool description","what the AI is told the tool does."),("Tool result","what comes back after running it.")],
notit='The AI doesn\'t run the tool or access your systems directly. Your code decides whether and how to run each request, which is where safety checks belong.',
take=["Write clear tool descriptions; the AI chooses tools by reading them.","Check every tool request before running anything risky.","Return clear results, including clear errors."],
close="The AI asks. Your software acts.",
q="Which tool would you give your AI first?",
scene='"What\'s the weather in Pune right now?"\nThe AI can\'t know. Its knowledge stopped at training.',
hook="So it asks your app to run a weather tool. That's tool calling.",
extra="The AI asks. Your software acts."),

dict(n=64, slug="mcp", title="Why Is Everyone Talking About MCP?", topic="MCP",
buzz='"MCP." "Model Context Protocol." It went from unknown to everywhere in under a year. What problem does it solve?',
hw='Before USB, every printer, camera and keyboard had its own plug. Then USB: one standard plug for everything.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **MCP** is a standard plug between AI apps and tools or data. Build a connector once, and any AI app that supports MCP can use it.',
step='Without MCP, every AI app needed its own custom code for every tool: Slack, Google Drive, a database. With MCP, a tool provider builds one MCP server, and any compatible AI app can connect to it and discover what it offers.\n\nAnthropic introduced MCP in late 2024, and it\'s now supported widely across AI tools. It makes connecting easy; deciding what\'s safe to connect is still your job.',
met='AI apps and coding tools with a list of "connectors" or "MCP servers" you can switch on.',
name='**MCP**, the Model Context Protocol.',
words=[("MCP server","a connector that exposes a tool or data source."),("MCP client","the AI app that connects to servers."),("Protocol","an agreed way of talking.")],
notit='MCP isn\'t a security system or an AI model. A connected tool can still leak data or take wrong actions. What MCP doesn\'t solve is the theme of my Series 11.',
take=["Think of MCP as USB for AI tools.","Review third-party MCP servers before connecting them.","Give each connection only the access it needs."],
close="One standard plug for AI tools.",
q="Which tool would you plug into your AI first?",
scene='Before USB, every device had its own plug.\nThen: one plug for everything.',
hook="MCP is that one plug, between AI apps and tools.",
extra="Connecting gets easy. Deciding what's safe is still yours."),

dict(n=66, slug="multi-agent-system", title="Are Several AI Agents Better Than One?", topic="Multi-agent system",
buzz='"Multi-agent systems." Teams of AI agents working together. It sounds like a sci-fi office.',
hw='Planning a wedding alone is overwhelming. So you have a coordinator, a caterer, a decorator and a photographer, each an expert, with the coordinator keeping everyone in sync.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: a **multi-agent system** splits a big task among several specialised AI agents (write-up 61), usually with a coordinator.',
step='A coordinator agent breaks the goal into parts and hands them out. Specialist agents work on their part with their own instructions and tools, sometimes in parallel. The coordinator combines the results.\n\nIt helps for big, varied tasks. But every hand-off can lose context or pass on errors, and several agents cost more than one. Often a single agent does the job.',
met='Research tools that send several agents to read different sources at once. Coding tools with separate planner, coder and reviewer roles.',
name='A **multi-agent system**.',
words=[("Coordinator","the agent that splits and assigns work."),("Sub-agent","a specialist agent doing one part."),("Hand-off","passing work or results between agents.")],
notit='More agents isn\'t more intelligence. Start with one agent; split only when the task truly needs it. (Series 15 covers the pitfalls.)',
take=["Try one agent before building a team.","Give each agent a clear, narrow job.","Check results at every hand-off."],
close="A wedding planner and a team. Only if the wedding is big enough.",
q="Would you build one smart agent or a team of small ones?",
scene='Planning a wedding alone is overwhelming.\nSo: a coordinator, a caterer, a decorator, a photographer.',
hook="A multi-agent system is that team, made of AI agents.",
extra="Powerful for big jobs. Overkill for small ones."),

dict(n=67, slug="ai-memory", title="How Does an AI Remember You?", topic="AI memory",
buzz='"Now with memory." Chat apps advertise that the AI remembers you. But write-up 28 said models don\'t learn from chats. So how?',
hw='A good hotel remembers you prefer a quiet room and green tea. The staff didn\'t change their brains; they wrote it in your guest file and check it when you arrive.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **AI memory** saves useful facts about you outside the model and puts them back on its desk (write-up 15) in future chats.',
step='As you chat, the app picks out facts worth keeping ("vegetarian", "works in finance", "prefers short answers") and stores them. Next time, it fetches the relevant ones and adds them to the prompt. Some apps also keep summaries of past conversations.\n\nThe model itself is unchanged. Memory is a filing system around it, which means it can store wrong or outdated facts too.',
met='Chat apps that say "memory updated". Assistants that remember your name, preferences or ongoing projects.',
name='**AI memory**, also called **long-term memory** or **personalisation**.',
words=[("Short-term memory","the current chat on the AI's desk."),("Long-term memory","facts saved across chats."),("Memory retrieval","fetching the right saved facts when needed.")],
notit='It isn\'t the AI learning or retraining. And it should be visible: good apps let you see, edit and delete what\'s remembered. (Series 12 covers memory pitfalls.)',
take=["Check what your AI apps remember about you.","Delete outdated or sensitive memories.","For company assistants, decide what should never be remembered."],
close="Not a better brain. A guest file.",
q="Do you know what your AI remembers about you?",
scene='A good hotel remembers your quiet room and green tea.\nNot magic. A guest file.',
hook="AI memory is that guest file, read back at the start of each chat.",
extra="The model doesn't change. The file does."),

dict(n=69, slug="copilot", title="What's the Difference Between a Copilot and an Agent?", topic="Copilot",
buzz='"Copilot." Microsoft, GitHub and many others use the word. It\'s become the name for AI that helps you work.',
hw='A copilot in a plane suggests, checks and helps, but the pilot stays in charge and decides.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: an AI **copilot** works alongside you, suggesting drafts, code or answers, while you review and decide. An agent (write-up 61) goes further and acts on its own.',
step='Copilots live inside the tools you already use: suggesting the next line of code, drafting an email reply, summarising a meeting. You accept, edit or ignore each suggestion.\n\nMany products are now moving from copilot to agent: from "here\'s a suggestion" to "I\'ve done it, please check". The more it acts alone, the more checks you need.',
met='GitHub Copilot suggesting code, Microsoft Copilot in Office apps, AI writing help in email.',
name='An AI **copilot**.',
words=[("Copilot","AI that assists while a person stays in control."),("Suggestion","an output you accept, edit or reject."),("Human in the loop","a person reviewing before anything happens.")],
notit='A copilot isn\'t a replacement for the person, and its suggestions aren\'t automatically right. Reviewing them is part of the job.',
take=["Treat copilot suggestions as drafts.","Notice when a tool moves from suggesting to acting.","Keep review steps for anything important."],
close="The copilot suggests. The pilot decides.",
q="Do you use a copilot at work? Does it suggest or act?",
scene='In a plane, the copilot suggests and checks.\nThe pilot decides.',
hook="An AI copilot suggests. You decide. An agent acts.",
extra="Know which one you're using."),

dict(n=70, slug="agent-sandbox", title="Where Should an AI Agent Do Its Work?", topic="Agent sandbox",
buzz='"Run it in a sandbox." It\'s the standard safety advice for AI agents that write and run code. What is the sandbox?',
hw='A child learning chemistry gets a toy lab kit in a tray, not the keys to the school\'s chemical store. If something spills, it stays in the tray.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: an **agent sandbox** is a walled-off computer where an AI agent can run code and use tools without touching your real systems.',
step='When an agent writes code, installs packages or browses websites (write-up 68), anything could go wrong: a bad command, a malicious web page, a runaway loop. In a sandbox, the damage stays inside: separate files, limited network access, no real passwords.\n\nNewer designs give each agent its own small virtual computer that can pause while the agent waits (my Today in AI No. 010 covers Google\'s approach).',
met='Coding assistants that run code in a separate environment. "Code interpreter" features that run your data analysis safely.',
name='An **agent sandbox**.',
words=[("Sandbox","an isolated space for running untrusted actions."),("Isolation","keeping the sandbox separate from real systems."),("Least privilege","giving only the access needed.")],
notit='A sandbox isn\'t permission to give the agent everything inside it. If you put real passwords or production data in the sandbox, they\'re at risk too. (Series 16 covers what agents should be allowed to do.)',
take=["Run agent code in a sandbox, never on your main machine with your logins.","Keep real passwords outside the sandbox.","Limit network access to what the task needs."],
close="Spills stay in the tray.",
q="Where do the AI agents in your team run code today?",
scene='A child\'s chemistry kit sits in a tray.\nIf it spills, it stays in the tray.',
hook="An agent sandbox is that tray, for AI agents running code.",
extra="Real systems and passwords stay outside."),
]
